{
 "id": "TD-06",
 "system": "ac3",
 "sessionId": "bm-ac2-td-06-f555c1c208",
 "endReason": "escalated",
 "customerTurns": 5,
 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Indesit dryer making a horrible squealing noise when it starts up, goes away after a few mins. Still dries fine"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is jockey / idler tension pulley. With the dryer unplugged and empty, turn the drum slowly by hand. Does it turn smoothly and quietly, or does it feel rough, rumble or squeak as you turn it?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "It turns ok but there's a slight squeak/scrape and it feels a little rough as it goes round."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "What is the noise like - a squeal/chirp, a rhythmic thump, a scrape, or a rattle - and does the drum still turn?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "It's a high-pitched squeal, mainly for the first few minutes of a cycle, then it quietens down. The drum still turns fine, no knocking or thumping."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Coins, buttons, zips and hair grips are the usual cause. With the dryer unplugged, check the drum and its lifters, the lint filter and the slot it sits in, and turn the drum slowly while you listen. Did you find something (and have you removed it), can you see something stuck that won't come out, or is it all clear?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "No, I looked in the door seal and the filter and couldn't find anything stuck. Nothing in the drum either."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Run a short programme. Has the noise gone, or is it still there?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I haven't run one yet since I only checked the drum and filter. The squeal still happens on and off, mostly on cold mornings, and it's worse with a heavy load. Should I run a short cycle now?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "No, you should not run the machine as it is making a horrible squealing noise. Continuing to use it could cause further damage to the bearings or motor. From what you have checked, this needs an appliance engineer to look at it: the next steps are beyond the checks that are safe to do yourself."
  }
 ],
